headline: Setting up a scene

task: Add a matrix sequence and a rotation matrix (eulerAngleY) to a scene. Drop rotation matrix into sequence and set it's value to 30 degrees. 

hint: *Right click -> Transformations -> rotate -> eulerAngleY*

![eulerAngleY](seq_rotate.png)

task: Add an inversion and transpose operators to scene. Connect sequence with rotation to them.

hint: *Right click -> Operator -> matrix -> inversion / transpose*

![Inversion/Transpose](invtrans.png)

--
headline: Rotation 

Once everything is ready, try connecting the inversion to sequence first, and then try the transpose.

![Connect](connect.png)

Interesting isn't it ? The result is same, but we are preforming two different operations.

--
headline: Scale

Now let's try the same process but with a different transformation matrix.

task: Add scale matrix instead of rotation matrix and set it's value to 2. After that, try connecting the operators in the same way you did before.

hint: *Right click -> Transformations -> scale*

![Scale](scale.png)

--
headline: Moment of truth

For a scale matrix inverse and transpose are not equal.

Why is it works for rotation and not for scaling ?

Answer is that rotation matrix is *orthogonal* and scale matrix is not.

--
headline: Orthogonality

In linear algebra, an *orthogonal matrix* or *orthonormal matrix Q*, is a real square matrix whose columns and rows are orthonormal vectors. 

A set of vectors is orthonormal if every vector in the set has a *length of 1*, and every vector is *perpendicular* to every other vector in the set.

Because we have those properties we can see that : 

![Inverse = Transpose](transinv.png)

--
headline: Rotation VS Scaling

They both pass ortoghonality test (all vectors in matrix are perpendicular to every other vector in matrix) but scale matrix *fails* the "normal" test. 

Vectors in scale matrix aren't normalized (their length is not equal to 1). Because it fails even one of the two rules, it is not orthonormal.

!["Normal" test](xyz.png)

